Discovery device inventory authority
===================================

A discovered MAC address is an identifier, not permission to reassign a device
from another tenant. Both the preload lookup and the unique-insert conflict
lookup restrict devices to active sites in the captured organization. The
MAC-less lookup also requires an authorized site. Discovery within one tenant
retains the existing controller-reassignment behavior.

A controller must have an active, undeleted site and organization before adapter
access. After adapter I/O, discovery rechecks the controller and current tenant
sites. It holds shared PostgreSQL locks on the controller, site and organization
authority rows through the Device inventory commit. Invalid, foreign, missing,
deleted or inactive resolved target sites are refused before inventory changes.
A concurrent insert collision with no tenant-scoped winner aborts and rolls back
the inventory transaction, returning discovery_device_scope_conflict without
loading or modifying the other tenant's device. The existing controller status
and history are not overwritten by this safe refusal.

Aggregate discovery now reports success:false when any selected controller
returns failure or raises an exception. controllers_failed includes exceptions;
processed/successful/failed counts remain accountable. A no-controller run is
still an explicit successful no-work outcome. Partial work by successful
controllers is retained; this is not an all-controller transaction.

Compatibility and limits
------------------------

The global live-MAC unique index remains unchanged. Identical nonempty MACs
cannot yet be represented independently across tenants; discovery safely refuses
the conflict. Supporting that requires separate schema and ownership migration
work. Unscoped/orphan inventory does not gain a tenant through MAC matching.
Explicit invalid UUID site mappings retain the existing resolver's fallback to
the controller default; the resolved target is always checked for authority.

These locks protect the Device inventory transaction, not the preceding adapter
operation or later deep-sync/ZTP work after commit. The initial per-controller
request does not receive the original caller's complete grant snapshot. Changes
between job selection and entering the per-controller routine require separate
execution-authorization work. A later deep-sync failure still has its existing
reporting behavior. Device/controller events are still published by their
existing transport; they are not a transactional outbox, and a late rollback can
leave a prior event without committed inventory. Global discovery lock ownership,
raw controller error persistence and internal Celery diagnostics remain separate
work. Old workers retain the original behavior: update workers consistently.

Shared authority locks can briefly delay site/controller/organization edits;
they are acquired after adapter I/O, not across the external discovery request.
This does not establish a multihost availability or hardware-write guarantee.

Verification
------------

Real PostgreSQL regressions cover preloaded foreign MACs, a foreign insert
committed after the preload SELECT, invalid site mappings, authority changes
during adapter I/O, and independent updates blocked by transaction locks.
Legitimate same-tenant discovery/controller reassignment remains covered.
Separate-process probes exercise actual committed database effects while adapter,
deep-sync, ZTP and event edges are synthetic. They do not contact hardware.
